From: nuc-bios-build <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] UefiPayloadPkg: give the onboard NIC a UNDI/SNP driver

Nothing in this payload publishes EFI_SIMPLE_NETWORK_PROTOCOL for the onboard
I218-V. UefiPayloadPkg carries prebuilt Realtek and ASIX UNDI blobs and no Intel
one, and SnpDxe only layers SNP over an existing UNDI/NII instance -- so the
BMC's CDC-ECM gadget ends up being the only network interface the firmware
exposes. Measured on hardware 2026-08-04:

    NucRedfishSync: 2 SNP handle(s) in the system
    NucRedfishSync:   SNP[0] DA:A7:62:23:3E:F5
    NucRedfishSync:   SNP[1] DA:A7:62:23:3E:F5

Both are the gadget; the LOM (B8:AE:ED:7E:3F:6E) is absent.

That is what breaks a chainloaded iPXE snp.efi, which is what Tinkerbell boots.
snp.efi contains no native drivers -- it binds SNP handles and nothing else --
so it saw the management link as its only interface, retried DHCP on it, and
gave up at the retry limit. No amount of interface ordering helps when there is
only one interface, and the ordering itself is not adjustable anyway: the ECM
hangs off the xHCI controller at 00:14.0, which precedes the LOM at 00:19.0 in
PCI order, and both are fixed Wildcat Point-LP function numbers that
devicetree.cb describes rather than assigns.

So publish an SNP for the LOM. The driver is iPXE built as a UEFI driver
(bin-x86_64-efi-sb/intel.efidrv, via interface/efi/efi_snp.c) rather than an
application, dispatched from the DXE FV alongside the Realtek and ASIX blobs it
sits next to. Its driver set is the intel driver alone, so it carries no USB or
SNP shims and cannot bind the gadget itself.

Gated on NETWORK_IPXE because that is what stages the binary.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [platform specific]
Signed-off-by: nuc-bios-build <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadPkg.fdf
+++ b/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadPkg.fdf
@@ -504,6 +504,15 @@
 !if $(ARCH) == IA32
   # FIXME
 !else
+# NucRedfish: UNDI/SNP for the onboard LOM. Nothing else in this payload
+# publishes EFI_SIMPLE_NETWORK_PROTOCOL for it, so a boot client that binds
+# SNP handles only -- iPXE's snp.efi, which Tinkerbell chainloads -- saw just
+# the BMC's host interface and had nothing to netboot from.
+!if $(NETWORK_IPXE) == TRUE
+  FILE DRIVER = F773F4F9-5C3E-475F-AE02-151D514D7379 {
+    SECTION PE32 = UefiPayloadPkg/NetworkDrivers/ipxe-intel.efidrv
+  }
+!endif
 !if $(NETWORK_RTEK_PCI)
   FILE DRIVER = D30D5496-6DF9-462D-9B97-0B93F659D1A0 {
     SECTION PE32 = UefiPayloadPkg/NetworkDrivers/RtkUndiDxe.efi
